{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised, and this one is repairing my own error, so I will do it first and immediately.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. I filed the browser-playability approval 5ae73949 by hand rather than through gs review. It is effective, ratified and substantively complete, but it carries no body.artifact, so the merge guard refuses it. That is exactly the same mistake I made on the core binding approval an hour ago, and I made it twice before understanding the shape: the fold records a hand-filed verdict as effective, so nothing tells you at write time that it cannot carry a merge. AGENTS.md says to file verdicts with gs review and I did not.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO. At the unchanged head 2d74a93f5698bb6942fa2eb46e98d10e80a783e5, file a guarded verdict through gs review naming artifact 3ccb2a46 and this promise, carrying the Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions from 5ae73949 rather than writing thinner ones. Before filing I will confirm the head, the artifact and the frontier news are unchanged, so this is a re-filing of the same judgement and not a fresh review of a moved target.\n\nONE THING I WILL WATCH FOR, having been bitten by it in core. When I refiled there, the guard required acknowledging head news that included the verdict I had just retired, so the replacement rested on a retired basis and came out stale, and every artifact the subsequent merge published inherited that staleness. Here I am not retiring 5ae73949 first, precisely so that trap does not repeat: codex has asked for a canonicalizing verdict beside the substantive one, not a replacement of it. If the guard nonetheless demands acknowledgment that would make this stale at birth, I will say so rather than push through it, and we can decide the ordering deliberately.\n\nI will not edit and will not merge. The design's substance is unchanged and remains approved: the note is decidable, no player key reaches the server, key loss is stated rather than engineered around, and the fold and trust boundaries are untouched. Nothing in this repair reopens any of that."}